WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.238
Level 3 supports site-wide operations and coordination across production areas.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:18.676
Common functions include operations management, manufacturing execution, batch or recipe management, site historians, engineering services, OT administration, and other systems needed to run the facility as a whole.

00:00:18.676 --> 00:00:26.647
A historian may collect process data from several areas for trending, quality analysis, reporting, and investigation.

00:00:26.647 --> 00:00:32.797
A manufacturing execution system can coordinate work orders, materials, and production status.

00:00:32.797 --> 00:00:36.213
Batch systems manage recipes and sequences.

00:00:36.213 --> 00:00:40.540
Engineering workstations maintain controller logic and device configurations.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:46.234
These functions have broader scope than a single HMI or controller.

00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:54.205
Level 3 systems are often conventional servers and workstations, but their operational context changes their risk.

00:00:54.205 --> 00:00:59.444
An engineering workstation may have authority to modify many controllers.

00:00:59.444 --> 00:01:04.682
A compromised operations server may disrupt coordination across several lines.

00:01:04.682 --> 00:01:14.931
A historian can become a pathway toward lower levels or a bridge toward enterprise consumers if its connections are not controlled.

00:01:14.931 --> 00:01:27.002
Security priorities include privileged-access management, secure administration, application allowlisting where appropriate, tested backups, patch planning, centralized logging, and segmentation between production areas and higher-level networks.

00:01:27.002 --> 00:01:34.973
Service accounts and vendor tools deserve particular attention because they may have broad and persistent access.

00:01:34.973 --> 00:01:41.123
Level 3 is also a natural place to coordinate monitoring and response.

00:01:41.123 --> 00:01:47.272
It can provide context about production schedules, engineering changes, and expected traffic.

00:01:47.272 --> 00:02:01.621
During an incident, the organization must understand which site services can be isolated, which are required for safe operation, and how lower-level control can continue if site coordination is degraded.
